  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Why Isn't My Grout Sealer Working?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
    "url": "http://coloradoremodeling.company"
  },
  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you put in the effort to seal your grout, only to find it's not actually doing its job. Usually, the main culprits are sealing too early, applying it over grout that isn't completely clean or dry, or using the wrong type of sealer for your grout. For cementitious grout, which is pretty common, you'll want a penetrating sealer. If you seal too soon, before the grout has fully cured (some manufacturers even say wait up to seven days!), the sealer can't bond properly. Plus, any dirt or moisture trapped under the sealer will just prevent it from working effectively. It’s also possible you didn’t wipe off the excess sealer from the tile faces, leading to a haze that makes it look like it’s not sealing. If you're still scratching your head after trying these tips, or if your grout has some serious staining or cracking, it might be time to bring in a pro. They can assess the situation and recommend the best fix, especially in high-moisture areas where proper sealing is absolutely crucial.",
